# 2. Intake, Assessment, and Diagnosis (NCE content outline, 12%): 24 items
CAT = "nceAssess"; SEG = "ASM"
Q = [
("During a mental status exam, a client knows her name, where she is and the date. This shows she is:",
 "Oriented times three", ["Showing intact insight", "Showing good judgment", "Free of thought disorder"],
 "Orientation to person, place and time is often recorded as oriented x3. Insight and judgment are separate parts of the mental status exam."),
("In a mental status exam, a client's report that he feels empty is recorded as his:",
 "Mood", ["Affect", "Thought content", "Perception"],
 "Mood is the client's own report of sustained emotion. Affect is what the counselor observes in expression, such as flat or tearful."),
("A client's speech jumps from topic to topic with loose links between ideas. This describes a problem with:",
 "Thought process", ["Thought content", "Orientation", "Psychomotor activity"],
 "Thought process is how ideas connect, such as flight of ideas or loose associations. Thought content is what the client thinks about, such as delusions or obsessions."),
("A test given twice to the same group two weeks apart produces similar scores. This shows good:",
 "Test-retest reliability", ["Content validity", "Split-half reliability", "Predictive validity evidence"],
 "Test-retest reliability is consistency of scores over time. Split-half reliability compares two halves of one administration."),
("A college entrance test's scores are compared with students' later grades. This checks the test's:",
 "Predictive validity", ["Concurrent validity", "Face validity", "Inter-rater reliability"],
 "Predictive validity is a form of criterion validity in which test scores are compared with a criterion measured later. Concurrent validity uses a criterion measured at the same time."),
("A client's z-score on an anxiety scale is +1.0. About what percentile is this on a normal curve?",
 "84th", ["68th", "50th", "95th"],
 "Half the scores fall below the mean and about 34% more fall between the mean and one standard deviation above it, so z = +1.0 sits near the 84th percentile."),
("A T-score of 70 on a clinical scale is how many standard deviations above the mean?",
 "Two", ["One", "Three", "Seven"],
 "T-scores have a mean of 50 and a standard deviation of 10, so 70 is two standard deviations above the mean."),
("A client scores 85 on a test of intelligence with a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15. Her score is:",
 "One SD below the mean", ["Two SDs below the mean", "One SD above the mean", "Exactly at the mean"],
 "85 is 15 points, one standard deviation, below a mean of 100. About 16% of people score at or below that point."),
("A counselor wants a brief screen for depression symptoms over the past two weeks in primary care. Which tool fits?",
 "PHQ-9", ["GAD-7", "AUDIT", "Strong Interest Inventory"],
 "The PHQ-9 screens for depression using the nine DSM criteria over the last two weeks. The GAD-7 screens for anxiety and the AUDIT for alcohol use."),
("Which instrument is a broad measure of personality and psychopathology with validity scales?",
 "The MMPI", ["The Beck Depression Inventory", "The Self-Directed Search", "The Bender-Gestalt test"],
 "The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory assesses many clinical scales and includes validity scales that detect random or defensive responding."),
("A counselor asks a client the four CAGE questions. What is being screened?",
 "Alcohol misuse", ["Suicide risk", "Cognitive decline", "Eating problems"],
 "CAGE asks about cutting down, annoyance at criticism, guilt and an eye-opener drink. Two or more yes answers suggest problem drinking."),
("A client says she has thought about killing herself. What should the counselor do next?",
 "Ask about plan, means and intent", ["Move on to a lighter topic", "Avoid using the word suicide at all", "Wait until the next session"],
 "Asking directly about plan, access to means and intent does not plant the idea; it guides the risk assessment and safety planning."),
("Which factor is generally the strongest single predictor of a future suicide attempt?",
 "A previous attempt", ["Being married", "A stable full-time job", "Regular physical exercise"],
 "A history of a suicide attempt is among the strongest predictors of a later attempt. Marriage and employment are usually protective."),
("A counselor uses the DSM-5-TR Cultural Formulation Interview. Its main purpose is to:",
 "Learn how the client sees the problem", ["Rule out every medical cause", "Estimate the client's level of intelligence", "Shorten the standard intake"],
 "The Cultural Formulation Interview asks how the client and their community understand the problem, its causes, supports and help-seeking, so diagnosis and treatment fit the client's culture."),
("A client's sudden confusion, poor attention and fluctuating awareness began over two days during a fever. This most suggests:",
 "Delirium", ["Major neurocognitive disorder", "Major depressive disorder", "Dissociative amnesia"],
 "Delirium has an acute onset, fluctuates and impairs attention, often from a medical cause. A neurocognitive disorder usually develops gradually over months or years."),
("Before diagnosing an anxiety disorder in a client with a racing heart and weight loss, the counselor should:",
 "Refer for a medical evaluation", ["Start exposure therapy at once", "Diagnose panic disorder", "Ask about career goals"],
 "Medical conditions such as hyperthyroidism can mimic anxiety, and DSM-5-TR asks that symptoms not be better explained by a medical condition. A medical check comes first."),
("A client's depressive symptoms are found to come from a prescribed steroid. Which DSM-5-TR diagnosis applies?",
 "Medication-induced depressive disorder", ["Major depressive disorder, single episode", "Persistent depressive disorder", "Adjustment disorder with depressed mood"],
 "When a substance or medication causes the mood symptoms, DSM-5-TR uses a substance/medication-induced depressive disorder rather than a primary depressive disorder."),
("Which describes an informal assessment?",
 "Watching the client's body language", ["Giving a standardized inventory", "Scoring a norm-referenced test", "Using a published symptom rating scale"],
 "Informal assessment includes observation, unstructured interviewing and client self-report without norms. Standardized tests are formal assessments."),
("A counselor gives a symptom scale at intake and again at session ten. This is mainly done to:",
 "Measure counseling outcomes", ["Meet an insurer's billing rule", "Diagnose a new disorder", "Test the client's memory span"],
 "Pre-test and post-test measures show change over time, which helps evaluate counseling effectiveness and revise the treatment plan."),
("A test's score has a standard error of measurement of 3. A client scores 50. About 68% of the time, the true score falls between:",
 "47 and 53", ["44 and 56", "49 and 51", "41 and 59"],
 "One SEM on either side of the observed score gives a band that holds the true score about 68% of the time, so 50 plus or minus 3 is 47 to 53."),
("Which level of care fits a client who is safe at home but needs several hours of treatment most days?",
 "Intensive outpatient program", ["Inpatient hospitalization", "Monthly outpatient sessions", "Residential treatment program"],
 "Intensive outpatient programs give several hours of structured treatment several days a week while the client lives at home. They sit between outpatient care and partial hospitalization."),
("A client reports drinking more than intended, failed attempts to cut down and cravings, but no other criteria. How severe is the alcohol use disorder?",
 "Mild", ["Moderate", "Severe", "Below the threshold"],
 "DSM-5-TR rates substance use disorders by the number of criteria met in 12 months: 2 to 3 is mild, 4 to 5 moderate, 6 or more severe. Three criteria is mild."),
("At intake, a couple argues and each blames the other while the counselor watches. Noting this pattern is an example of:",
 "Evaluating interactional dynamics", ["Administering a formal test", "Taking a full family medical history", "Completing a career assessment"],
 "Watching how family members or partners interact in session reveals patterns, such as blaming and pursuing, that formal tests miss."),
("A trauma screen shows a client has symptoms 10 days after a car crash. Which diagnosis may fit this time frame?",
 "Acute stress disorder", ["Posttraumatic stress disorder", "Adjustment disorder only", "Persistent depressive disorder"],
 "Acute stress disorder covers symptoms from 3 days to 1 month after a trauma. Posttraumatic stress disorder requires symptoms lasting more than a month."),
]
